[[
    Priority 7/10 : Add a better switch that always compiles down to an if-chain unless break is used in any case
                    ^^ (compatibility reasons)
        |> then it falls through as a normal C-switch
        |> this also includes for cases to handle things such as:
            case 0 => 10: ...
        |> '=>'  is the "inclusive-exclusive" range-operator (..  in Rust)
        |> '==>' is the "inclusive-inclusive" range-operator (..= in Rust)
        |> things such as:
            switch (x)
            {
                case isspace(_):
                    foo();
                default:
                    bar();
            }
        |> where _ is treated as the implicit argument, is replaced by the actual switch argument during codegen
]] -> DONE


Priority 2/10 : Making an LSP for C+

Priority 5/10 : Adding small generic support in C+
    |> instead of using template<...>, C+ uses specify<...>
    |> example:
        specify<typename T>
        T add(T l, T r)
        {
            return l + r;
        }
        int main()
        {
            add:<int>(3, 6);
        }
    |> at the call-site, the generic syntax will result in being the "SMALLFISH" (:<...>)
    |> Types can have constraints via this, though constraints are only for what fields/methods a struct/type has:
        specify<typename T : T::StringInto(_)>
        void print(T to_print)
        {
            std::print(to_print.StringInto());
        }
    |> The above shown _ again means that the compiler doesn't need to care what arguments are passed into StringInto
    |> You can specify them, e.g. typename T : T::StringInto(void *t, Formatter *f), or whatever, but _ is more general
    |> Mangling for generics will be done using monomorphization and also using __ as the seperator between function name
    |> and the generic arguments, e.g. add:<int> will be mangled into add__int, add:<int, float> will be mangled
    |> and the output will be add__int__float


